---
epr-meta-version: 1
id: a2o-stewardship-custodial
covers: subtree
purpose: >
  The custodial account class — a PARTY acting on ANOTHER party's account under a witnessed,
  revocable, expiring grant. Neither side is necessarily one person: the steward may be an
  individual guardian, a household pair, a care home, a correctional institution, a court, or a
  qahal acting by community consensus; the subject is a child, a legal ward, an adult with IDD,
  a senior, or anyone under organizational device management. The substrate already models most
  of it (imagodei `StewardshipGrant`/`StewardshipAppeal`/`DevicePolicy`, plus
  `Collective`/`Membership`/`CollabAgreement` in the same zome); nothing above the DNA does, and
  `steward_id` is still a single String while `authority_basis` already names institutional
  bases. Stewardship is also PLURAL: many parties hold authority over one person at once, each over
  a different dimension (parents day-to-day, a school in school hours, a physician for health data),
  composing socially rather than by rank — no primary-steward slot, and restrictions composing as an
  order-independent UNION because every layer may only ADD. These scenarios are where that model
  gets exercised and validated, and they are the FIRST deliverable of the lane — the two-party carrier is designed against a passing scenario,
  not ahead of one. Concern taxonomy, the collective two-hop path, and the pickup order live in
  this directory's README.md; the parent genesis/a2o/.epr-meta carries the blind-reader review
  obligation that every `.feature` write here inherits.
rules:
  - id: grant-scenario-must-exercise-the-appeal
    class: inject
    when: { write: "*.feature" }
    dedupe-of: genesis/a2o/features/stewardship/README.md (concern taxonomy + the appeal path)
    why: >
      A stewardship scenario that shows only the steward acting has modeled a G Suite admin
      console, not this protocol. `StewardshipGrant` carries `appeal_id` and
      `StewardshipAppeal` carries four appeal types (`scope`, `excessive`, `invalid_evidence`,
      `capability_request`) because the SUBJECT has standing to contest the grant — and the
      subject here is frequently a person least able to assert it unaided. Two invariants the
      zome enforces that a scenario must not quietly contradict: authority is RELATIONAL
      (over this subject's named surfaces — never a rank, never a `PermissionLevel` tier), and
      `DevicePolicy` composes ONE-WAY (a layer may only ADD restrictions, never remove a
      parent's). Prefer a scenario where the grant is exercised AND contested; if a scenario
      genuinely covers only the steward's side, say in a comment which sibling scenario
      carries the appeal, so the omission is a decision rather than a drift.
      WHEN THE STEWARD IS A COLLECTIVE (a household pair, a care home, a correctional
      institution, a court, a qahal): the obligation intensifies rather than dilutes, because the
      asymmetry is larger and the subject is least able to assert their own standing. Two extra
      assertions belong in such a scenario — authority must reach the acting human over a
      traversable path (`StewardshipGrant.steward_id` = Collective CID → `Membership{role:
      Steward}` → the member), and the ACTING MEMBER must be identifiable afterwards. An
      institution that can act on a person's account without naming the human who acted is the
      failure mode this whole directory exists to prevent. Compose on `Collective`/`Membership`
      (same integrity zome) and p2p-design-gate before proposing any new entry type — the honest
      answer may be zero.
    retire-when: >
      when this directory has at least one green appeal-path scenario AND the two-subject
      carrier exists above the DNA (a `StewardshipGrant`-aware doorway session) — at that
      point the appeal is load-bearing in code and a scenario that drops it fails rather than
      needing a reminder.
cites:
  - genesis/docs/content/elohim-protocol/architecture/2026-08-25-doorway-auth-posture-declared-stage.md
  - genesis/data/timeline/backlog/commons-holonic-stewardship-backlog.md
---
# stewardship/ — the custodial account class

`.feature` files here carry `@concern:custodial-stewardship-<nn>-<name>` (taxonomy table in
`README.md`) and exercise the imagodei stewardship entry types across the steward↔subject seam.

**One rule, narrowly scoped.** Following the `resiliency-saga/` precedent, authoring discipline
(tags, RED-FIRST, step placement) stays prose convention in `README.md` and the parent
`CLAUDE.md`. The single injected rule above exists because the drift it catches is *specific,
already-observed at design time* (the admin-console framing drops the appeal path), and it is
born with a retirement condition rather than as permanent furniture.
